# Sub-tick interpolation of the homeworld portal preview's aperture
# (tests/harness/check_portal_subtick.sh).
#   <bk2> | <V> | vertex=<x>,<y> ... [tol=<px>] [step=<px>] [judge=<a>,...]
# V is the canonical VBL the intermediate present at V+1 sits above; V-2 opens
# the interval that present covers. V is even because a tick spans two VBLs
# here: the intermediate present at V+1 has to fall inside V's own tick.
#
# The statistic is the aperture polygon's corners, read from the feeder's own
# emitted list, not a pixel mask: the aperture spans most of the frame.
# Spyro flies at the Skelos Badlands portal and the aperture opens across these
# ticks. Each row names three corners of the near portal's aperture whose
# canonical step over the interval is well clear of the floor and that stand
# far apart from every other corner. Corners sliding along the frame's bottom
# edge are left out: they run tens of pixels per tick because they are the
# intersection of two nearly parallel lines, and their motion prices the clip
# rather than the aperture.
#
# The second row is the interval whose portal set changes (the near portal's
# visible pentagon edge count drops), where the whole-set lerp gate used to
# disable interpolation for every portal of the present.
#
# The bound is 1.0 px: the rect is captured as integers and the aperture is
# their clip against the pentagon, so a corner's expected position carries the
# rounding of both.
autumn-plains-skelos-badlands-portal-entry.bk2 | 1096 | vertex=472.17,12 vertex=403,216 vertex=92.62,228 tol=1.0
autumn-plains-skelos-badlands-portal-entry.bk2 | 1100 | vertex=491.27,12 vertex=69.71,228 vertex=0,131.02 tol=1.0
